  "full_response": "Garbage Collection (GC) in Java and manual memory management in C++ represent two fundamentally different approaches to memory management, each with its own trade-offs. Here’s a detailed comparison:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Garbage Collection in Java**\nJava uses an **automatic garbage collector** to manage memory, relieving developers from manually allocating and deallocating memory. The JVM (Java Virtual Machine) handles memory reclamation through a process called **garbage collection**.\n\n#### **Key Features of Java GC:**\n- **Automatic Memory Management**:\n  The JVM automatically identifies and reclaims memory occupied by objects that are no longer reachable (i.e., no references point to them).\n  Example:\n  ```java\n  Object obj = new Object(); // Allocated on the heap\n  obj = null; // Now eligible for GC\n  ```\n\n- **Generational GC**:\n  The heap is divided into generations (Young Generation, Old Generation, and sometimes Permanent Generation/Metaspace). Objects are promoted from young to old based on their lifespan.\n  - **Young Generation (Eden, S0, S1)**: Short-lived objects are collected frequently (minor GC).\n  - **Old Generation**: Long-lived objects are collected less often (major GC/full GC).\n  - **Metaspace**: Stores class metadata (replaced PermGen in Java 8).\n\n- **GC Algorithms**:\n  Java uses different GC algorithms (e.g., Serial, Parallel, CMS, G1, ZGC, Shenandoah), each optimized for different workloads (throughput, latency, etc.).\n  Example: `-XX:+UseG1GC` enables the G1 garbage collector.\n\n- **Finalizers and Cleaners**:\n  Java provides `finalize()` (deprecated in Java 9) and `Cleaner` (Java 9+) for resource cleanup, but these are unreliable and slow. Prefer `AutoCloseable` with try-with-resources.\n\n- **No Explicit Deallocation**:\n  You cannot manually free memory (no `free()` or `delete` equivalent). The GC decides when to run.\n\n- **Stop-the-World (STW) Pauses**:\n  Most GCs pause application threads during collection (though modern GCs like ZGC/Shenandoah minimize this).\n\n- **Memory Leak Risks**:\n  While GC prevents dangling pointers, memory leaks can still occur (e.g., unintended object references in collections).\n\n---\n\n### **2. Manual Memory Management in C++**\n",
